<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">René of Anjou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1409">1409</a>–<a href="/years/1480">1480</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Good King René</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">René of Anjou was Duke of Anjou, Count of Provence and Duke of Bar, and by marriage Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, as well as titular king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He lost Naples to Alfonso of Aragon in 1442. Known as Good King René, he was a patron of art, letters and tournaments at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a> and Aix. After his death Anjou and Provence passed to the French crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou.jpg" alt="René of Anjou" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Froment · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_Roi_Ren%C3%A9.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
